    "content": "> [!warning] Scaffolding Removal Schedule\n> - **End of Part I (Gate 1)**: Diagnostic Ladder is removed. You must perceive structural hierarchy without a checklist.\n> - **End of Part II (Gate 2)**: 7-Layer Brief Template is removed. You must author bespoke specifications.\n> - **End of Part III (Gate 3)**: Pipeline exercises are removed. You author your own generative system.\n> - **Part VI & Beyond**: Prompts and assignments are removed. You begin from the Blank Sheet.\n\n\n---\n\n# Scaffolding Removal as Pedagogical Principle\n\n[[#Table of Contents|↑ Back to TOC]]\n\n> [!important] Formal Pedagogical Concept\n> **Scaffolding removal** is the course's central mechanism for producing independence. Every major transition removes a specific form of external structure. The removal is deliberate and sequenced. Scaffolds may be reintroduced deliberately when they serve the work, but the practitioner must remain capable without them.\n\n## The Scaffolding Sequence\n\n| Stage | What is supplied | What is removed |\n|---|---|---|\n| Part I | Exercise, ladder, template, references | — |\n| Part II | Exercise, template | Ladder |\n| Part III | Exercise | Template |\n| Part IV | Category | Exercise |\n| Part V | Situation | Category |\n| Part VI | Conditions only | Situation |\n| Month 4 | Brief | Conditions |\n| Month 5 | Category | Brief |\n| Month 6 | Theme | Category |\n| Month 7 | Real-world situation | Theme |\n| Month 8 | Nothing | All external input |\n| Month 9 | Nothing | Even the problem itself |\n\n## Scaffolding vs Structure\n\n> [!important] The Distinction\n> The goal is not for Month 9 to become *\"Here's a blank page. Good luck.\"*\n>\n> The goal is: **the learner supplies the structure.**\n\nThe real progression is:\n\n> **external structure → internalised structure → self-authored structure**\n\nA mature practitioner is usually **more structured internally**, not less.\n\nIndependence does not mean refusing templates, briefs, or tools. It means not being dependent on them. A professional can pick up a scaffold deliberately when it is efficient, and set it down when it is not.\n\n## What Scaffolding Removal Is Not\n\nIt is not abandonment. Each removal is preceded by practice with the scaffold present. The scaffold is removed only after the underlying capacity has been trained.\n\nIt is also not a test of grit. If the removal produces collapse, that is diagnostic — it tells you what to practise next, not what to give up on.\n\n## Why It Matters\n\n> [!question] The Decisive Question\n> **If the course disappeared tomorrow, would I still know how to begin?**\n\nA practitioner trained with permanent scaffolding can only work within scaffolds. A practitioner trained through scaffolding removal can work anywhere.\n\nThe removal is the course.\n\n---"
