    "content": "> [!important] The 5-Second Flash Rule\n> Human vision reads large silhouette masses in 100–200ms. If the 3 major masses fail to establish a focal hierarchy, no amount of 8K detail will save the image.\n\n\n---\n\n# Module V1 — Visual Observation & Diagnosis\n\n[[#Table of Contents|↑ Back to TOC]]\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild visual knowledge that makes visual diagnosis possible.\n\n## Required Observation Studies\n\nAcross Weeks 1–2, complete short studies in:\n\n- mass and silhouette\n- proportion and relative scale\n- gesture and directional force\n- perspective and spatial construction\n- value grouping\n- focal hierarchy\n- edge hierarchy\n- material differentiation\n- environmental relationships\n\nThese are not portfolio drawings. They are **perceptual calibration studies**.\n\n## Visual Diagnostic Ladder\n\n1. **Intent** — what should the image do?\n2. **Composition** — how does the frame organise attention?\n3. **Value / major shape** — does the image read as large masses?\n4. **Gesture / action** — what is the directional energy?\n5. **Construction** — do objects and figures occupy believable space?\n6. **Proportion** — are internal relationships convincing?\n7. **Subject-specific structure** — do anatomy / architecture / object systems hold together?\n8. **Material** — do surfaces behave coherently?\n9. **Rendering** — do edges, colour, detail, and texture support the above?\n\n## Visual Study Protocol\n\nFor one reference image:\n\n1. reduce it to three to five major masses\n2. identify the focal path\n3. identify the dominant directional axis\n4. redraw the construction underneath the surface\n5. reconstruct the image from memory\n6. compare the reconstruction against the reference\n7. state what you failed to perceive\n\nRepeat with generated images.\n\n> [!example] What a Visual Perception Study Looks Like\n>\n> **Reference:** A photograph of a crowded market.\n>\n> **Step 1 — Major masses:** I reduce it to five shapes: a vertical mass (the crowd), a diagonal mass (the awning), a horizontal mass (the table), a dark mass (shadow under the table), and a light mass (the sky opening).\n>\n> **Step 2 — Focal path:** My eye enters at the brightest point (the sky opening), moves down the diagonal of the awning, lands on the figure at the table, then spreads into the crowd.\n>\n> **Step 3 — Directional axis:** The dominant flow is left-to-right, following the crowd's movement.\n>\n> **Step 4 — Construction:** I redraw the underlying perspective — the vanishing point is slightly left of center, and the table sits on a plane that recedes more steeply than the crowd behind it.\n>\n> **Step 5 — Memory reconstruction:** I redraw from memory. My version compresses the crowd into a single mass, loses the sky opening, and flattens the table.\n>\n> **Step 6 — Compare:** The reference has more depth because the sky opening creates a recession. My version is flatter because I didn't perceive the recession.\n>\n> **Step 7 — What I failed to perceive:** The sky opening is not just brightness — it's a spatial device. It makes the market feel like it's in a courtyard rather than a street.\n\n## Transfer Test\n\nA random image is shown at thumbnail size for five seconds.\n\nReconstruct:\n\n- dominant mass arrangement\n- focal point\n- directional flow\n- approximate value grouping\n\nThen diagnose where your reconstruction differs from the source.\n\n---"
