    "content": "> [!important] Part I Completion Criteria\n> 15 Written Diagnoses with zero evaluative adjectives, 6 Visual Perception Massing studies, 3 Close-Reading Analyses, and 1 defended problem carried into Part II.\n\n\n---\n\n# Part I — Deliverables\n\n[[#Table of Contents|↑ Back to TOC]]\n\n## Core\n\n1. Personal visual and writing diagnostic ladders.\n2. 15 written diagnoses: 8 visual, 7 textual. (Reduced from 30 — quality over quantity.)\n3. 6 short visual perception studies.\n4. 3 close-reading analyses.\n5. One subject-position statement: what can you notice, care about, or decide that a default generation process may not supply?\n6. First anchor set: accepted and rejected examples across both media.\n7. One successful transfer test.\n8. Two standing-inquiry entries.\n9. Practice log with at least 6 sessions documented.\n10. Problem-finding log with at least 4 entries (including positionality field) and one selected problem carried forward.\n11. Non-instrumental attention log with at least 2 entries.\n12. Play log with at least 2 entries.\n\n> [!tip] On Quantity\n> The original course asked for 30 diagnoses. That is too many. Fifteen done well is better than thirty done perfunctorily. The goal is to build the habit of diagnostic attention, not to fill a quota.\n\n## Deepening (Optional)\n\n13. One exploratory study in a third mode (sound, time-based, spatial).\n\n## Part I Mastery Gate\n\nDemonstrate that you can:\n\n- distinguish observation from evaluation\n- identify the highest-level unresolved problem\n- name the decision behind the problem\n- propose a meaningful intervention\n- transfer the diagnostic process to unfamiliar material\n- **locate a problem worth pursuing without being assigned one**\n- **run a positionality check and refuse a problem you have no standing to pursue**\n- **sustain attention without instrumentalising it**\n- **make something for no reason at all**\n\n---"
