    "content": "> [!important] Part II Verification\n> Every brief dossier must show explicit rejection logic for at least 2 alternate premises.\n\n\n---\n\n# Part II — Deliverables\n\n[[#Table of Contents|↑ Back to TOC]]\n\n## Core\n\n1. Six premise records with rejection logic.\n2. Six fully specified briefs.\n3. 12–18 visual thumbnails and value studies. (Reduced from 18–24 — quality over quantity.)\n4. Four writing structural outlines. (Reduced from six.)\n5. Two generated/drafted iterations per brief minimum.\n6. Rejection records for failed premises and failed outputs.\n7. Two transfer tests.\n8. One selected premise that you continue into Part III.\n9. Practice log with at least 6 sessions documented.\n10. Problem-finding log continued (at least 4 new entries, with positionality).\n11. Non-instrumental attention log continued (at least 2 new entries).\n12. Play log continued (at least 2 new entries).\n13. **Six provenance records completed.**\n\n> [!tip] On Quantity\n> The original course asked for six premises, six briefs, and 18–24 thumbnails. That is a lot. The reduced numbers here are still substantial. If you find yourself rushing to meet a quota, you are defeating the purpose. It is better to produce four excellent premise records than six mediocre ones. The numbers are a guide, not a law.\n\n## Deepening (Optional)\n\n14. One cross-medium premise sketch (image + text arguing together).\n\n## Part II Mastery Gate\n\nDemonstrate that you can:\n\n- originate a premise rather than merely respond to a brief\n- distinguish premise from subject\n- write a specification that constrains decisions without over-determining them\n- explain why references are being used\n- state acceptance and rejection conditions before generating\n- produce a meaningful solution without relying on a reference solution\n- **record human contribution alongside tool contribution**\n\n> [!question] The Gate Question\n> Can you look at a premise you generated and say, honestly, \"This is mine — I found it, I chose it, I can defend it\"? If the answer is \"I'm not sure,\" the premise is not yet yours. Return to Module 2 or Module 5.\n\n---"
