    "content": "> [!important] Deliverable Standard\n> 1 visual pipeline, 1 writing pipeline, failure maps, and 12 controlled variations.\n\n\n---\n\n# Part III — Deliverables\n\n[[#Table of Contents|↑ Back to TOC]]\n\n## Core\n\n1. Two documented pipeline systems (one visual, one writing).\n2. Variable map for each system.\n3. Failure-point map for each system.\n4. 12 controlled visual variations. (Reduced from 20.)\n5. 8 controlled writing variations. (Reduced from 10.)\n6. One human-only visual study and one human-only writing study.\n7. One deliberate pipeline failure experiment per track.\n8. One transfer test performed without the documented pipeline visible.\n9. Practice log with at least 6 sessions documented.\n10. Problem-finding log continued (at least 4 new entries).\n11. Calibration log with at least 5 documented predictions (with confidence levels).\n12. Play log continued (at least 2 new entries).\n13. **Versioning and data policy documented for each tool used.**\n\n> [!tip] On Quantity\n> The original course asked for 20 visual variations and 10 writing variations. That is a lot. The reduced numbers here — 12 and 8 — are still substantial. The goal is not to produce a large quantity of variations. The goal is to understand what varies and why. Twelve variations you can explain are worth more than twenty you cannot.\n\n## Deepening (Optional)\n\n14. One deviation study — take a convention and break it deliberately.\n15. One physical-sustainability audit of your working setup.\n\n## Part III Mastery Gate\n\nDemonstrate:\n\n- causal understanding of your workflow\n- ability to predict at least some failures before generation\n- ability to recover from failures\n- ability to distinguish consistency from repetition\n- ability to work without the tool stack for a short period\n- understanding of which parts of the workflow are genuinely authorial decisions\n- **ability to record where the human contribution begins and ends**\n- **awareness of the physical load of sustained practice**\n\n> [!question] The Gate Question\n> Can you explain, in plain language, what your pipeline is doing and why each part of it exists? If you can only describe what you click, you do not yet understand the system. If you can describe what variable each step controls and what happens when it fails, you do.\n\n---"
